The report's45 NPZ archives are compared array-for-array with an untouched original run:
dtype, shape and every byte must match. Batch/source order, completed-epoch histories,
selection, fresh restore, explicit-noise repeatability, code-only decoding and inference
immutability are checked as well. A separate optional NumPy oracle checks mixture algebra,
all first VAE/D derivatives, first Adam updates, reference-head paths, predictions, all six
eligible criteria and fitting-only probes; it performs no training or data preparation.

```
python3 scripts/check_factor_tc_variational_autoencoder.py docs/mepml/examples/nn/2_autoencoders/2.2_variational_autoencoders/build/tc_vae --json build/nn-report-conversion/factor_tc_vae/oracle.json
```

Reusable C++ APIs are EmpiricalTotalCorrelation, FactorDiscriminator,
FactorDiscriminatorLoss, PermuteLatentColumns, FactorVariationalAutoencoder and
BetaTcVariationalAutoencoder. Python and R expose the same native components and test explicit
host optimization, live reference gradients and owning nested results after garbage collection.
They do not constitute complete independent host-language experiment ports. Per-column shuffle
ordering follows the current C++ standard-library implementation; it is not a cross-toolchain
bitstream promise. The reports remain the editable complete C++ experiment.
